{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Bridge git delivery is abandoned after ONE transport failure (TLS/DNS/timeout)\n\n## Impact, stated first\n\nTwo Reconcile Bridge runs **converged the reconcile** and then lost the delivery to\n`origin/tickets`, leaving unpushed commits on the runner that die with it:\n\n- run 31535962079: `push-transport-failed: fatal: unable to access\n  'https://github.com/navapbc/rebar/': server certificate verification failed. CAfile:\n  none CRLfile: none` → `(3 unpushed commits on origin/tickets..HEAD)` →\n  `Bridge delivery failed (exit 1)`.\n- run 31420498173: `merge-recovery-blocked: fatal: ... server certificate verification\n  failed` plus `fatal: could not fetch 983800f2 from promisor remote` →\n  `(2 unpushed commits)`.\n\nA converged pass whose events never reach `origin/tickets` is silently lost work; the\nheartbeat canary is the only detector.\n\n## Confirmed root cause\n\n`src/rebar/_store/push.py` retries **only** a genuine non-fast-forward. Every other\nnon-zero push is terminal on the FIRST failure:\n\n```python\nif not _is_non_fast_forward(stderr):\n    reason = (\n        \"push-policy-declined\" if _is_policy_decline(stderr) else \"push-transport-failed\"\n    )\n    _raise_if_strict(strict, reason, stderr, base_path, remote_ref)\n    return  # non-retriable class — best-effort\n```\n\nThe comment justifying \"terminal\" is about **permanent rule violations** (\"hitting the\nremote twice more cannot change a permanent rule violation\") — true for a policy decline,\n**false** for a transport fault. But both land in the same branch, so a TLS handshake\nblip, a DNS failure, or the 30 s `_GIT_TIMEOUT` (which surfaces as returncode 124 /\n`git timed out after 30s`) all get **zero** retries.\n\nThere is also **no backoff anywhere** in this loop; the existing non-FF retries fire\nback-to-back.\n\n`CAfile: none CRLfile: none` points at the runner's CA bundle not resolving — a hosted\nrunner infra flake, not a rebar bug. The `filter: blob:none` partial clone widens the\nblast radius, because on-demand promisor fetches during merge recovery hit the network\ntoo (hence the `could not fetch ... from promisor remote` in 31420498173). rebar cannot\nfix the runner's CA store, but abandoning a converged pass after a single transient blip\nis rebar's defect and is squarely fixable.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. Add a transport-retriable classifier in `src/rebar/_store/push.py` that recognises the\n   transient transport signatures observed in CI and their close relatives (TLS/certificate\n   verification, could-not-resolve-host, connection reset/timed out, the synthetic\n   `git timed out after 30s`, early EOF / RPC failures, promisor-remote fetch failure).\n   It must be **subtractive** in the same shape already proven for `_is_non_fast_forward`\n   (bug 2a76) and `_ref_lock` (bug 4afc): a policy decline can never be transport-retriable,\n   and ambiguity resolves to TERMINAL rather than to a retry loop that cannot converge.\n2. Add a bounded, injectable sleep helper. **Declared budget: 3 transport attempts total\n   per leg, with an attempt-indexed backoff of 0.5 s then 2.0 s** (the last delay repeats\n   if a leg ever gains more attempts). Small on purpose: ride out a blip, never sit on a\n   real outage while commits pile up.\n3. In the push loop, when the failure is transport-retriable and the transport-retry budget\n   is not spent, sleep and retry rather than raising. Keep the budget small and explicit.\n4. When the transport budget IS spent, raise exactly today's `push-transport-failed` reason\n   with the same detail + unpushed-commit summary, so the strict contract and the existing\n   reason vocabulary are unchanged.\n5. Leave `push-policy-declined` strictly terminal — unchanged, zero retries.\n6. Cover BOTH recovery legs, which fail with **different** reasons and must be named\n   separately:\n   - the **explicit fetch** in `_recover_non_fast_forward`, which raises\n     `push-transport-failed` (not `merge-recovery-blocked`); and\n   - the **promisor on-demand fetch that fires inside `git merge` itself** in\n     `_merge_remote_under_lock` — this is the leg that produced run 31420498173's\n     `merge-recovery-blocked: ... server certificate verification failed` together with\n     `could not fetch ... from promisor remote`, because the checkout is a `blob:none`\n     partial clone.\n   Both legs run the SAME subtractive transport classifier. A genuine merge conflict is\n   never transport-retriable and stays terminal on its first failure, still raising\n   `merge-recovery-blocked`.\n\n7. **Required structural consequence:** `src/rebar/_store/push.py` already sits at 786 of\n   the repo's 800-LOC hard cap, so this fix cannot land without a split. Move the\n   push-failure **classification, backoff and strict-reporting** cluster to a new\n   `src/rebar/_store/push_classify.py` along the seam those functions already form — they\n   answer \"what KIND of failure is this, how long do we wait, and how is it reported\", and\n   none of them shells out to git or touches the worktree. The dependency is one-way\n   (`push.py` imports `push_classify.py`), and the moved code is otherwise unchanged, so\n   most of this change's line count is relocation rather than new logic.\n\n## Scope / non-goals\n\n- **No** change to the reason vocabulary (`push-transport-failed`,\n  `push-policy-declined`, `final-push-rejected`, `merge-recovery-blocked` all keep their\n  spelling and meaning) — only *when* they are raised.\n- **No** change to `_MAX_RETRIES = 5` for the non-fast-forward path.\n- No new secrets, credentials, permissions, or infrastructure; no CI runner changes. Fixing\n  the runner's CA bundle is explicitly out of scope — this ticket makes rebar survive the\n  flake, and is deliberately a reliability-only change.\n- Does not touch the ref-lock CAS classifier (`freeborn-dizzy-raven`) or the workflow\n  re-dispatch step (`callous-wavy-kite`).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: A test proves a push failing with the exact CI TLS stderr (`server certificate\n  verification failed. CAfile: none CRLfile: none`) is retried and then **succeeds**,\n  where today it raises on the first failure.\n- [ ] AC2: A test proves the transport retry budget is bounded — a persistently failing\n  transport push stops after exactly **3** attempts and raises `push-transport-failed`,\n  carrying the git stderr and the unpushed-commit summary.\n- [ ] AC3: A test proves a policy decline (`pre-receive hook declined` / `GH013`) is still\n  terminal after exactly ONE push attempt and still raises `push-policy-declined` — the\n  bug 2a76 contract is not weakened.\n- [ ] AC4: A test proves retries sleep between attempts (via the injected sleep seam),\n  i.e. they are not back-to-back.\n- [ ] AC5: The existing push contract tests still pass unchanged, including\n  `tests/unit/test_push_strict.py::test_five_rejections_spend_one_terminal_push_and_earlier_success_does_not`\n  (the 6-push non-FF budget) and the transport/policy reason parametrisation.\n- [ ] AC7: A test proves the **merge** leg retries a transport fault — a recovery merge\n  failing with the run-31420498173 TLS + `could not fetch ... from promisor remote` stderr\n  is retried and then succeeds, where today it raises `merge-recovery-blocked` on the\n  first failure.\n- [ ] AC8: A test proves a genuine merge conflict is NOT retried as a transport fault —\n  it stops after ONE merge and still raises `merge-recovery-blocked`.\n- [ ] AC6: `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass, the complexity ratchet reports no new\n  debt, and every `src/rebar` module stays under the 800-LOC hard cap."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786527664186001001,"uuid":"a4c4f6b2-efbd-4929-8cc7-8ec29e0a423a"}